.TH "UBERTOOTH\-EGO" "1" "October 2026" "Project Ubertooth" "User Commands"
.SH "NAME"
\fBubertooth\-ego\fR \- Yuneec E\-Go sniffing
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.nf
ubertooth\-ego \-f
.fi
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
The Yuneec E\-Go is an electric skateboard controlled by a wireless handheld remote\. This handheld remote uses a custom protocol implemented in 2\.4 GHz RF\. ubertooth\-ego(1) can sniff this protocol and interfere with its operation\.
.SH "EXAMPLES"
Sniff the connection between an E\-Go and its remote:
.IP "" 4
.nf
ubertooth\-ego \-f
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
Receive all E\-Go packets on 2408 MHz:
.IP "" 4
.nf
ubertooth\-ego \-r \-c 2408
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
Interfere with a connection:
.IP "" 4
.nf
ubertooth\-ego \-i
.fi
.IP "" 0
.SH "OPTIONS"
Major modes:
.TP
\fB\-f\fR
Sniff a connection by hopping along channels
.TP
\fB\-r\fR
Continuously receive packets on a single channel (see \fB\-c\fR)
.TP
\fB\-i\fR
Interfere with a connection
.TP
\fB\-j\fR
Original "jam" spelling of \fB\-i\fR; does exactly what \fB\-i\fR does
.P
Options:
.TP
\fB\-c <2402\-2480>\fR
Channel in MHz to use for continuous receive (suggested: 2408, 2418, 2423, 2469; default: 2418)\. Only \fB\-r\fR uses it: \fB\-f\fR, \fB\-i\fR and \fB\-j\fR hop across the E\-Go's own channel list, so \fB\-c\fR with one of those is refused rather than ignored\. The default is sent like any other channel, so \fBubertooth\-ego \-r\fR on its own receives on 2418 MHz rather than on whatever channel the board was left on\.
.TP
\fB\-l <1\-48>\fR
Packet length to use\. The value must be a complete decimal integer from 1 through 48; invalid values fail before the Ubertooth is opened\.
.TP
\fB\-a <access_code>\fR
Access code to use, given as 2, 4, 6 or 8 hex digits (1 to 4 whole bytes)\. A code shorter than four bytes becomes the most significant bytes of the radio's 32\-bit sync word and the rest are zero, so \fB\-a 1122\fR matches \fB11220000\fR, not a shorter pattern\. See ACCESS CODE below for what a run without \fB\-a\fR uses\.
.TP
\fB\-U <index|serial>\fR
select an Ubertooth by enumeration index or serial number; see ubertooth(7), SELECTING A DEVICE
.SH "ACCESS CODE"
\fB\-a\fR sets the access code on the board, which keeps it until the board is reset or reflashed\. A run without \fB\-a\fR sends no access code at all, so it uses whichever one the board was last given, not a default of its own:
.IP "" 4
.nf
ubertooth\-ego \-r \-c 2418 \-a 11223344   # sets the board's access code
ubertooth\-ego \-r \-c 2418               # still receives with 11223344
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
\fB630f9ffe\fR is the firmware's value after a reset, so it is what a run without \fB\-a\fR uses only on a board that has not been given another\. Pass \fB\-a\fR when a run has to be reproducible, or reset the board (\fBubertooth\-util \-r\fR) first\. All three modes use the access code\.
.SH "TRANSMIT POWER"
Interference (\fB\-i\fR, \fB\-j\fR) transmits at the CC2400 power level set with \fBubertooth\-util \-a\fR and leaves it unchanged\. A board that has not been given a level jams at 7 (0 dBm, the maximum), the CC2400's reset value and the level jamming always used before\. Sniffing does not transmit and does not change the level\. These levels are at the CC2400 radio; the Ubertooth One's RF amplifier changes the actual output after that\.
.SH "ARGUMENTS"
\fBubertooth\-ego\fR takes options only\. A word that is not an option, including a bare \fB\-\fR, is refused before any device is opened and named: \fBerror: unexpected argument '<word>'\fR, with exit status 1\.
.P
\fB\-h\fR ends the command only once the whole command line has parsed, so a stray word or a bad option value is reported even alongside it\.
.P
An option value must be a whole value in its range, or it is refused the same way instead of being read as 0, for example \fBerror: channel must be 2402\-2480 MHz (got 2481)\fR\.
.P
A major mode is the work, so a command line without \fB\-f\fR, \fB\-r\fR or \fB\-i\fR is refused before any device is opened: \fBerror: nothing to do: choose a major mode (\-f, \-r or \-i)\fR\. It used to open the selected board, check its firmware, do nothing and exit 0\.
.P
Each major mode runs until it is interrupted, so only one of them can run: two in one command are refused before any device is opened, naming both as they were spelled, \fBerror: \-r cannot be combined with \-f; choose one of \-f, \-r or \-i\fR\. \fB\-i\fR and \fB\-j\fR are two spellings of the jamming mode, so they clash too\. The last mode written used to be the one that ran, and the others were dropped without a word\.
.P
\fB\-c\fR outside continuous receive is refused before any device is opened too, naming the mode as it was spelled: \fBerror: \-c cannot be combined with \-f; only continuous receive (\-r) uses a channel\fR, with no usage text after it (see DIAGNOSTICS)\. It used to be accepted and then dropped silently in those modes\.
.SH "DIAGNOSTICS"
Standard output carries only the captured packets\. Errors, warnings and progress go to standard error, each error as one line starting \fBerror:\fR, for example \fBerror: E\-GO not supported by this firmware\fR\.
.P
A request that sets the capture up and is refused by the board ends the command there, named in one line, for example \fBerror: could not set the channel\fR; libubertooth has already said why\. A refused request is not proof that the board never got it, so the message does not claim one way or the other\.
.P
A command line the tool will not take is refused before any device is opened, with exit status 1 and one \fBerror:\fR line on standard error and nothing after it: an option the tool does not know (\fBerror: unrecognized option '\-Z'\fR), one whose value is missing (\fBerror: option '\-c' requires a value\fR), a bad option value, a stray word, \fB\-c\fR outside continuous receive, and a command line that said something but has nothing to do\. The line describes the problem, so the usage text is left to \fB\-h\fR\. A bare \fBubertooth\-ego\fR, with no arguments at all, is the one refusal the usage text does follow, on standard error with the \fBerror:\fR line\.
.P
\fB\-h\fR is what the user asked for, so the usage text goes to standard output and nothing goes to standard error\.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
0 when the command did what was asked, including \fB\-h\fR, and after a capture is interrupted (Ctrl\-C)\. 1 for a command line the tool will not take or with nothing to do, when the selected Ubertooth is not found or cannot be opened, when the board refuses one of the requests that set the capture up, when the firmware does not support the requested mode, and when the capture stops short because a poll failed\.
